<h2 id="workouts">Sled Workouts for Speed</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Resisted sprints:</strong> 6 × 20 yards at 10–20% bodyweight, with 90 seconds to 2 minutes of rest.</li>
<li><strong>Contrast sprints:</strong> After your sled reps, do 4 × 20 yards with no sled so your body carries the power into free running.</li>
<li><strong>Heavy marches:</strong> Slow, powerful steps with a heavy load. This is drive-phase strength work, done early in the week.</li>
<li><strong>Push sprints:</strong> With a push sled, do 4–6 × 15 yards at a moderate load, holding a 45-degree lean.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do sled work twice a week with 48 hours between sessions, and pair it with heavy squats. Our <a href="/squat-strength-sprint-speed">squat strength and sprint speed guide</a> explains why the two work best together. Want explosive power too? See the <a href="/best-plyo-boxes-vertical-jump">best plyo boxes</a> for jump training.</p>
<h2 id="mistakes">Common Sled Mistakes</h2>
<p><strong>Too much weight.</strong> If your sprint turns into a march, you're training strength, not speed. Lighten it.</p>
<p><strong>A strap that's too short.</strong> A short tow strap jerks the sled on every stride and can clip your heels. Give yourself plenty of strap.</p>
<p><strong>Doing it tired.</strong> Resisted sprints are speed work. Do them fresh, early in the session, with full rest.</p>
<p><strong>Skipping the unloaded sprints.</strong> The free sprints right after the sled are where the speed carryover happens.</p>
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">How much weight should I put on a sprint sled?</div>
<div class="faq-a">For sprint speed, load the sled with 10–20% of your bodyweight. At that load you should still run at roughly 80–90% of your top speed with good mechanics. A 185 lb athlete would use about 18–37 lb. Heavier loads build acceleration strength but slow you down a lot, so treat them as strength work, not sprint training.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Is a sled better than a parachute for speed?</div>
<div class="faq-a">For the first 10–20 yards, yes. A sled gives you steady resistance from the first step and keeps you in a forward lean, which is exactly what the drive phase needs. A parachute adds resistance only once you're moving fast, so it's better suited to top-speed work.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Do sprint sleds work on grass and turf?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Yes, but the surface changes the resistance. The same plates feel much heavier on grass than on turf, and a sled on concrete or asphalt will scrape and slide differently. Set your load by how much it slows you down on your surface, not by the number of plates alone.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Do I need weight plates for a sprint sled?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Most sleds are plate-loaded, so you need plates that fit the sled's post. Some take both standard 1-inch and Olympic 2-inch plates. If you don't own plates, a sand-filled resistance bag like the SKLZ SpeedSac is the easiest option.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">How often should I do sled sprints?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Twice a week, with at least 48 hours between sessions, is enough for most athletes. Do 6 × 20 yards with full rest, then follow with a few unloaded sprints so the strength carries over to free running.</div>
</div>
